⚠ THE CRD YAML IS AT charts/kube-ovn/templates/kube-ovn-crd.yaml. There is no `yamls/crd.yaml` and no
`charts/kube-ovn/crds/` on master, and the Go types are NOT in a single `types.go` — they are split
per kind under pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/. Both are worth writing down because both cost a search.

── Scope ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

EVERY kind this family touches is CLUSTER-SCOPED. From vpc.go:

    // +kubebuilder:resource:scope="Cluster",shortName="vpc",path="vpcs"

and the same shape on Subnet (`shortName="subnet"`, `path="subnets"`), SecurityGroup
(`shortName="sg"`, path="security-groups", singular="security-group"), IptablesEIP
(`path="iptables-eips"`) and OvnEip (`path="ovn-eips"`).

⚠ THE CRD FILE DEFINES 25 kubeovn.io/v1 KINDS AND EXACTLY ONE IS NAMESPACED — VpcEgressGateway. That
is the whole list of exceptions.

⚠ SecurityGroup's plural is `security-groups`, HYPHENATED, not `securitygroups`. The cluster-backed
conformance harness derives its CRD stub's path from GroupVersionKind.Plural, so a hand-written
plural that guessed would produce a definition at a path the apply never reaches.

── Defaults and webhooks: there are almost none, and that matters ────────────────────────────────

Across Vpc, Subnet, SecurityGroup, IptablesEIP and OvnEip there are exactly TWO `+kubebuilder:default`
markers in the whole set:

    Vpc.spec.bfdPort.enabled       // +kubebuilder:default=false
    VpcDns.spec.corefile           // +kubebuilder:default=vpc-dns-corefile

Subnet: ZERO. SecurityGroup: ZERO. IptablesEIP: ZERO. OvnEip: ZERO. There is also no `required:` and
no `enum:` anywhere in the vpcs / subnets / security-groups / iptables-eips / ovn-eips schemas — every
spec field is optional and every string is free-form.

⚠ A TRAP THAT WAS CHECKED SO NOBODY HAS TO CHECK IT AGAIN. Grepping `default:` in the Subnet and Vpc
schemas HITS — and every hit is a property NAMED `default`, not a default value. Verbatim from the
Subnet schema:

              default:
                description: Whether this is the default subnet.
                type: boolean

That is `SubnetSpec.Default bool json:"default"`. A reviewer who greps and stops has the opposite of
the truth.

⚠ THERE IS NO MutatingWebhookConfiguration ANYWHERE IN THE PROJECT. pkg/webhook/webhook.go defines
only `type ValidatingHook`, whose create/update/delete hooks return `Allowed("bypass")` or
`Denied(...)` and never a patch. The one ValidatingWebhookConfiguration
(`pod-ip-validating.kube-ovn.io`) is OPT-IN AND OFF BY DEFAULT: dist/images/install.sh contains no
webhook at all, the v2 chart gates it on `validatingWebhook.enabled: false`, and yamls/webhook.yaml is
a separate manual apply with `failurePolicy: Ignore`. It does not cover security-groups, ovn-eips or
vpc-dnses at all.

⚠ SO CLIENT-SIDE VALIDATION IS THE ONLY VALIDATION, which is part of why this provider's
address-space check exists at all.

── Why Matches is containment anyway: the CONTROLLER writes to .spec ─────────────────────────────

This is the finding. The usual argument for containment — "the CRD defaults fields in" or "a mutating
webhook rewrites them" — is FALSE here, and containment is still mandatory, because the controller
issues a full Update against an object the platform applied.

pkg/controller/vpc.go, formatVpc():

    if item.Policy == "" { item.Policy = kubeovnv1.PolicyDst; changed = true }
    if route.Action != kubeovnv1.PolicyRouteActionReroute { if route.NextHopIP != "" { route.NextHopIP = "" } }

plus a finalizer, then `Vpcs().Update(...)`. And handleDeleteVpcStaticRoute REMOVES entries from
`vpc.Spec.StaticRoutes` outright.

pkg/controller/subnet.go, formatSubnet() and formatAddress():

    if newSubnet.Spec.Provider == "" { newSubnet.Spec.Provider = util.OvnProvider }          // "ovn"
    if newSubnet.Spec.Vpc == "" { ... newSubnet.Spec.Vpc = c.config.ClusterRouter }          // "ovn-cluster"
    if newSubnet.Spec.GatewayType == "" { newSubnet.Spec.GatewayType = GWDistributedType }   // "distributed"
    if newSubnet.Spec.EnableLb == nil && ... { newSubnet.Spec.EnableLb = &c.config.EnableLb }
    subnet.Spec.Protocol = util.CheckProtocol(subnet.Spec.CIDRBlock)                          // ALWAYS

    formatCIDR:       each comma-separated element through net.ParseCIDR, written back as ipNet.String()
    formatGateway:    derived from the CIDR when empty; in Dual a missing family is appended
    formatExcludeIPs: APPENDS every gateway IP and sort.Strings() in place

⚠ THE `spec.Vpc == ""` BRANCH IS THE MOST DANGEROUS LINE IN THIS FILE. An unbound Subnet is not
inert — it is assigned to the CLUSTER ROUTER, which is the platform's own default VPC. That is why
NetworkSubnets.VpcRefOf throws rather than rendering an empty string.

⚠ AND `formatCIDR` IS WHY NetworkSubnets.Matches COMPARES PARSED NETWORKS. A tenant who sends
10.20.1.7/24 has 10.20.1.0/24 stored, by the controller, one pass later. A string comparison reports
drift on a converged subnet forever.

── Dual-stack ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

`cidrBlock` is COMMA-SEPARATED, IPv4 FIRST. util.CheckProtocol splits on `,` and returns Dual only
when exactly two entries parse as one v4 and one v6; one entry gives IPv4 or IPv6; anything else gives
"". `gateway`, `excludeIps` entries and `u2oInterconnectionIP` follow the same convention.

Protocol constants (pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/subnet.go): ProtocolIPv4 "IPv4", ProtocolIPv6 "IPv6",
ProtocolDual "Dual", and — on master only, absent in v1.15/v1.14 — ProtocolMac "Mac".

── Server-side apply: every array here is ATOMIC ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

None of Vpc.spec.staticRoutes, Vpc.spec.policyRoutes, Vpc.spec.vpcPeerings, Vpc.spec.namespaces,
Vpc.spec.extraExternalSubnets, Subnet.spec.excludeIps, Subnet.spec.namespaces,
Subnet.spec.allowSubnets, SecurityGroup.spec.ingressRules or SecurityGroup.spec.egressRules carries an
`x-kubernetes-list-type` or `x-kubernetes-list-map-keys` marker, and there are no `+listType=` /
`+listMapKey=` Go markers. An array with no listType defaults to ATOMIC under SSA, so the last applier
owns the whole list.

⚠ THIS IS WHY THERE IS NO `routeTables` RESOURCE TYPE. Two platform resources cannot each own a slice
of one Vpc's static-route list; the second apply replaces the first's entries wholesale.

The only `x-kubernetes-list-type` lines inside the vpcs block belong to
`bfdPort.nodeSelector.matchExpressions` — controller-gen boilerplate for the embedded
metav1.LabelSelector. The set/map markers elsewhere in the file all belong to vpc-egress-gateways, the
one hand-marked CRD.

── There is no route-table object ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

No RouteTable, no VpcRouteTable, no top-level StaticRoute kind. A "route table" in Kube-OVN is a bare
STRING NAME referenced from Vpc.spec.staticRoutes[].routeTable and Subnet.spec.routeTable. It has no
independent existence, no lifecycle and nothing to observe.

⚠ RouterLBRule and SwitchLBRule are OVN LOAD-BALANCER rules. The names mislead; they are not routing
tables.
